; Minimal probe for stringCase (contract entry 36): `upper` / `lower`, one
; node. The corpus reached these only inside `testDiffStdlib`, a fixture that
; carries five members plus the math builtins, so a failure there could not be
; attributed to this node.
;
; ASCII-only is deliberate, not lazy. The contract's reading is Unicode-aware
; (`ADR-019 D1`); the LLVM emitter's is a byte-wise ASCII-only pass; and this
; harness asserts the two channels agree byte-for-byte. A non-ASCII receiver
; would therefore fail the harness because of a filed B-group deviation, which
; would say nothing about this node -- and a fixture that fails for a reason
; filed elsewhere is a fixture nobody can read. The deviation cannot be pinned
; here at all: proving it needs a test that asserts the two channels *differ*,
; which is a different harness, not a different fixture.
;
; The contract also requires the **receiver to be unchanged**, so the receiver
; is printed after the calls rather than only its results.

main|func() -> ():
    var s = "Hello, World"
    print(s.upper())
    print(s.lower())
    print(s)
    print("".upper())
    print("".lower())
    print("MiXeD 123".lower())
    print("MiXeD 123".upper())
    return
